        "diagnostics": [
          {
            "message": "Semicolon present; review for clause stacking.",
            "reason": "semicolon",
            "scope": "grid_storage.body",
            "text": "Electric-power construction growth increased materially to 10.8%. The active interconnection queue stands at 2,061 GW, and only 24.2% is in executed-agreement or construction stages; storage accounts for 552.7 GW of the queue. S&P Global reports that data centers are adding to transmission congestion. Faster construction is a meaningful counterweight, but most requested capacity has not reached an advanced stage."
          },
          {
            "message": "Semicolon present; review for clause stacking.",
            "reason": "semicolon",
            "scope": "macro.paragraphs[1]",
            "text": "The investment cycle retains internal financial support: operating cash flow covers current CapEx 1.42 times. Yet cash reserves equal 0.96 years of CapEx, borrower strain stands at 26.0 and lender strain is -13.5. The physical side has a similar split. Electric-power construction grew 10.8%, but only 24.2% of the 2,061 GW active interconnection queue has reached an executed-agreement or construction stage. Spending and construction are active; the harder question is how much proposed capacity can clear financing and grid connection."
          },
          {
            "message": "Semicolon present; review for clause stacking.",
            "reason": "semicolon",
            "scope": "macro.paragraphs[2]",
            "text": "Execution is also increasingly local. Available records contain 1,556 sites in development, about half the 3,068 operating sites. Construction Dive reports that public support is emerging as a data-center bottleneck, while the drought screen now places 24.4% of campuses with county data in counties containing some D2-or-worse drought; 22.2% are in counties where at least a quarter of the area is affected. County overlap does not establish site-level drought or water demand, and these developments do not show how many projects will be delayed. They reinforce why development-site counts should not be treated as completed capacity."
          }
        ],
